{"timestamp": "2026-01-12T22:56:18.759698", "level": "INFO", "event": "heuristic_reflection_completed", "data": {"experience_lessons": "The previous logic of sorting INA candidates solely by total traffic load (sum of job sizes traversing each switch) failed to handle spatially uneven job distributions, leading to overloading of individual switches in high-traffic Pods while underutilizing switches in low-traffic Pods. This caused severe processing capacity violations at specific ToR switches (e.g., ToR(165), ToR(163)) despite the INA budget being underutilized in other regions. Furthermore, the worker assignment heuristic of prioritizing shortest path without considering switch load or Pod-level load balancing created a 'hotspot' effect: multiple workers were routed to the same top-ranked INA switch even when it exceeded its 750 Gbps processing limit, resulting in overloads and wasted INA capacity. The assignment rule also failed to consider that INA switches should be deployed closer to worker clusters to reduce cross-Pod traffic and avoid core-layer congestion, especially when jobs span multiple Pods. This caused critical saturation at core links (e.g., Core(178) → Aggr(172)) in cross-Pod scenarios. These failures stem from the absence of load-aware placement and assignment, treating INA as a purely topological optimization rather than a resource-balancing mechanism.", "functions_to_update": ["_determine_ina_placement_candidates", "_assign_workers_to_aggregation_points"]}}
